#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167690} :activity/id #uuid "e6fad03d-8252-5f6b-bab3-01bfcf630b77" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048412 :topic/id #uuid "9ca98c7f-c00f-528a-8e93-a55c3adc20eb" :topic/math-academy-id 3272} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167569 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166743 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166581 :question/id #uuid "9ef5b1bc-547e-521c-8082-ad7c333942b6" :question/math-academy-id "e-6046"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e40869c1-4d5a-57af-a2a1-55b514614c4f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050990}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167318}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Bayes' Theorem With the Law of Total Probability"} :step/id #uuid "be3bb8fa-f933-597c-8ab7-4cc1bede75c0" :step/math-academy-id 22340 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167570}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167570 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166744 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166582 :question/id #uuid "789393d5-e431-577a-a2e8-660c9ac7e06b" :question/math-academy-id "e-6047"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b11a5ad-5289-55e6-9a97-4c4544d76673" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167317}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Accuracy of Medical Tests"} :step/id #uuid "bd0d23d0-f76e-54dd-812e-785b3882837e" :step/math-academy-id 22341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167690 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168081 :tutorial/content "For two events $A$ and $B$, **Bayes’ theorem** states that\n\n$$\nP(A \\mid B)=\\dfrac{P(B \\mid A)P(A)}{P(B)}\n$$\n\nDeriving Bayes' theorem is straightforward. First, recall that the multiplication law states that\n\n$$\nP(A \\mid B) = \\dfrac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(B)}.\\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nSwapping $A$ and $B$ in the multiplication law and noting that\n\n$$\nP(B\\cap A) = P(A\\cap B)\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\nP(B \\mid A) = \\dfrac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(A)}\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as\n\n$$\nP(A\\cap B) = P(B \\mid A)P(A)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above expression for $P(A\\cap B)$ into $(\\ast)$ gives Bayes' theorem.\n\nWe'll discuss the significance of Bayes' theorem shortly. But for now, let's get some practice applying the formula." :tutorial/id #uuid "4e1d5dc7-a377-56f0-b01f-288366126e34" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5146 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "605ba248-4688-5c95-a847-7027c1b70f03" :step/math-academy-id 32380 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167715}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167715 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166849 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166699 :question/id #uuid "c6061854-22f4-5b1e-889e-fc81c86cfe3b" :question/math-academy-id "e-8914"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12feb6dc-fadb-5502-86ed-7a238a3d38f1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050108}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167056}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability Using Bayes' Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "95f6f8a2-630a-5cd6-829a-d55dd3f9f4df" :step/math-academy-id 33290 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167771}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167716 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166850 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166700 :question/id #uuid "bf811787-2434-5ad8-9d8a-1ac9f823e1c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-8923"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "616b7aa2-2c55-5a29-a908-545a4bad4376" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329667}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Bayes’ Theorem to a Situation in Context"} :step/id #uuid "101d861a-7130-578c-bdab-ae7991d6567d" :step/math-academy-id 33311 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167770}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167770 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168135 :tutorial/content "It's possible to express Bayes' theorem in an alternative form using the law of total probability.\n\nFirst, let's restate Bayes' theorem:\n\n$$\nP(A \\mid B)=\\dfrac{P(B \\mid A)P(A)}{P(B)}\n$$\n\nNow, the law of total probability tells us that\n\n$$\nP(B) = P(B \\mid A)P(A) + P(B \\mid A')P(A')\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Bayes' theorem, we get\n\n$$\nP(A \\mid B)=\\dfrac{P(B \\mid A)P(A)}{P(B \\mid A)P(A) + P(B \\mid A')P(A')}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1f7cc012-6b04-5ce7-a959-822f139b1557" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6467 :tutorial/title "Formulating Bayes' Theorem From the Law of Total Probability"} :step/id #uuid "deefdc0d-d6d4-58f9-bdae-c680ade68f54" :step/math-academy-id 39079 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167569}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167771 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168136 :tutorial/content "Bayes' theorem states that\n\n$$\nP(A \\mid B)=\\dfrac{P(B \\mid A)P(A)}{P(B)}\n$$\n\nFor reasons that will become clear shortly, we'll rewrite Bayes' theorem in a slightly different (but equivalent) way, as follows:\n\n$$\nP(A \\mid B)=P(A) \\cdot \\dfrac{P(B \\mid A)}{P(B)}\n$$\n\nBayes' theorem is significant because it allows us to update our belief that a particular hypothesis is true when new evidence comes to light. For this reason, it has numerous applications in science, computer science, and engineering.\n\nFor example, suppose a doctor wishes to assess the probability that a certain patient is infected with a particular disease. It is known that those aged $65$ or older have a higher chance of carrying the disease.\n\nLet's define the events $A$ and $B$ as follows:\n\n- $A=$ the event that a randomly selected patient has the disease\n- $B=$ the event that a randomly selected patient is $65$ years old or more\n\nThen, Bayes' theorem gives the following:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{P(\\textrm{has disease} \\, \\mid \\, \\textrm{patient is} 65+)}} = {\\color{purple}{P(\\textrm{has disease})}} \\cdot \\dfrac{P(\\textrm{patient is} 65+ \\, \\mid \\, \\textrm{has disease})}{P({\\textrm{patient is} 65+})}\n$$\n\nLet's introduce some new terminology:\n\n- ${\\color{purple}{P(\\textrm{has disease})}}$ is called the **prior.** It tells us the probability that a randomly selected patient has the disease *without considering any other factors*.\n- ${\\color{blue}{P(\\textrm{has disease} \\, \\mid \\, \\textrm{patient is} 65+)}}$ is called the **posterior**. It tells us the probability that a randomly selected patient has the disease *taking into consideration* that they are $65$ or older.\n- $P(\\textrm{patient is} 65+ \\, \\mid \\, \\textrm{has disease})$ is called the **likelihood.** It tells us the proportion of infected people that are $65$ or older. This quantity could be established by taking a random sample from the population of infected people and computing the ratio of those that are $65$ or older.\n\nTherefore, Bayes' theorem allows us to update the hypothesis (the patient has the disease), taking into account information about the patient (they are $65$ or older), making use of existing available evidence (an estimate of the proportion of infected people that are $65$ or older).\n\nFurthermore, suppose the likelihood is updated at a later date (e.g., due to more evidence being available as more people become infected). In that case, this will further improve the accuracy of the posterior when future patients are assessed." :tutorial/id #uuid "dc2a4c3a-22a8-514b-af01-a3a5ce6e5725" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6468 :tutorial/title "The Significance of Bayes' Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "fb04ea52-bb84-520d-82c5-b791f49800a3" :step/math-academy-id 39080 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167716}}] :activity/title "Bayes' Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167035}]}
